<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petersberg Citadel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Petersberg Citadel is a fortress on a hill in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built from 1665 after the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> had brought the city under his control. It is one of the largest surviving baroque fortresses in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It was later held by Prussian and, during the Napoleonic period, French garrisons, and served as a barracks into the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petersberg-citadel.jpg" alt="Petersberg Citadel" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clemensfranz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zitadelle_Petersberg_Erfurt_Eingang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
